  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 409 PM EDT SAT SEP 19 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, eastern Kentucky will find \nitself in a region of largely zonal flow aloft, with a broad \ntrough over the northeastern CONUS and a closed upper level low to\nthe northwest in the Northern Plains. At the surface, a cold \nfront will push through the forecast area before losing momentum \nnear the TN line. Although the timing of this front remains in \nquestion, a temperature gradient looks to set up across the \nforecast area; north of the boundary, northerly winds will keep \nweather cooler, with high temperatures remaining seasonable (upper\n70s to low 80s), while south of the boundary, southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow will dominate. This will keep weather feeling \nmore like summer, with highs in the upper 80s or perhaps low 90s \nin areas unaffected by convection. That being said, the \naforementioned boundary, in addition to a shortwave trough \nembedded within flow aloft, will keep PoPs elevated, with at least\nscattered convection possible as early as Monday afternoon.\n\nThe pattern looks to be quite active from Monday evening onwards \nas the previously mentioned closed low progresses eastward and \neastern Kentucky finds itself under the influence of amplifying \ntroughing centered over the western Great Lakes. Each day, the \nenvironment will be moist (LREF mean PW values generally in the \n70th percentile or higher compared to climatology). There will be \nample forcing for convection as multiple embedded shortwaves \ntraverse the base of this trough, and with a surface low and \nassociated boundary lingering in the vicinity of the forecast area\nthrough at least Wednesday, rain chances should remain high \nthrough at least midweek. Ensemble guidance suggests moderate \ninstability remains favored to build each afternoon, and with high\nfreezing levels and a very saturated profile, any convection \ncould yield efficient rainfall rates. Flow will remain fairly weak\naloft, and thus minimal shear should preclude an organized severe\nweather risk; however, this unimpressive upper level flow will \nalso keep storm motion quite slow (generally in the 10-15 kt \nrange). Altogether, these factors indicate that the environment \nwill be favorable for flash flooding, particularly in areas that \nreceive multiple rounds of heavy rain. Given this, WPC has \nmaintained Day 3, 4, and 5 Marginal Risks (Level 1/4) for \nExcessive Rainfall across most of eastern Kentucky, with the \ncaveat that model spread remains high and a categorical upgrade \ncould be needed if/when confidence in these details increases. On \na positive note, this pattern should also provide some relief from\nthe heat; highs will generally be in the 70s across the forecast \narea, near to below average for this time of year.\n\nIn the latter half of the week, ridging will build over the \ncentral CONUS, with troughing now displaced east of our area. With\nthis more stagnant pattern in place, the trend will be towards \nless active weather, with only slight chance PoPs from Friday \nonwards. It will also feel more like fall; northerly flow should \nmaintain cooler temperatures, with highs in the low to mid 70s!",
